-- Version 3 → 4: an issue can be assigned to somebody.
--
-- One nullable column on `issues`, and nothing to back-fill: NULL is
-- "unassigned", which is the true and only possible answer for every issue that
-- predates the feature. See Issues.sq's assignee_id for what the column means
-- and why it is not composite-keyed against the project.
--
-- ── Why ALTER TABLE here, when 2.sqm's sibling had to rebuild ───────────────
--
-- Because this reference is single-column and nullable, which is exactly the
-- case SQLite's ALTER TABLE can do. `priority_id` could not be added this way —
-- it is NOT NULL and composite — so that migration rebuilt the whole table.
-- This one does not have to, and rebuilding anyway would be strictly worse: the
-- rebuild has to be done with foreign keys off (see Database.migrateSchema), and
-- a DROP TABLE issues with the constraints off is one typo away from taking
-- every comment, label and attachment in the database with it.
--
-- The `REFERENCES` clause survives ALTER TABLE, which is worth stating because
-- it is easy to assume otherwise: SQLite parses it into the table definition,
-- and `PRAGMA foreign_key_list` reports it afterwards — which is precisely what
-- MigrationTest compares, so a clause that had been silently dropped would fail
-- that test rather than sit undiscovered on the volume.
--
-- The default is NULL rather than stated, because SQLite REQUIRES that of a
-- column with a REFERENCES clause added by ALTER TABLE: any other default would
-- point every existing row at a user id that may not exist. Nothing to do here —
-- an omitted DEFAULT already means NULL — but it is the reason not to add one.
--
-- The new `be_assigned_issue` role needs no migration at all. `roles` is seeded
-- by RoleStore.seed() on every startup with INSERT OR IGNORE, so a volume that
-- has been serving for a month grows the row the first time this build boots.
-- See Roles.sq.

ALTER TABLE issues ADD COLUMN assignee_id INTEGER REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE SET NULL;
